**Meetings**

**Status Update 30.09.2026**

- Literature review table completed: 6 papers (Lu et al. 2026 survey; Borghesi et al. 2019 AICAS; Borghesi et al. 2019 AAAI; Bidollahkhani, Nordsiek & Kunkel 2026 anchor paper; Liu, Ting & Zhou 2008 Isolation Forest; Sun et al. 2026 EPC-TinyAD), in the format Michael requested (Study/citation, Domain/Data, Method, Key Findings, Limitation and Gap).

- Finalized proposal's Research Questions, for reference (see thesis_proposal.pdf, Material and Methods):

  - RQ1: Isolation Forest vs. Dense Autoencoder on GWDG GPU telemetry — detection quality (precision, recall, F1, lead time) and resource footprint (size, latency, memory) before compression.

  - RQ2: Effect of quantization/pruning on each baseline candidate — can they meet edge-representative resource profiles without unacceptable loss of detection quality.

  - RQ3: Resulting trade-off curve between detection performance and resource efficiency across models and compression levels — does the pre-compression ranking still hold after compression.

  - RQ4: Does detecting locally, on-node, offer a practical advantage over centralized detection when the monitoring pipeline itself is degrading? Rationale: GPU detachments degrade the very monitoring pipeline a centralized detector depends on, connecting the compression work (RQ1–RQ3) back to the motivating failure scenario.

- Jonathan raised follow-up questions on RQ4's "on-node vs. centralized" argument: exact mechanism of monitoring-pipeline degradation (local exporter losing the GPU vs. transport-path failure); how an on-node detector's alert reaches a human when the reporting path is also degraded; justification for local vs. centralized processing.

- Open item for RQ4 — to resolve with Michael: whether on-node detection's advantage should be framed as "detects before the pipeline degrades" (a lead-time race) rather than "still works despite degradation"; and confirming the scope boundary that RQ4/Phase 3 measures detection quality under degraded input, not whether the alert reliably reaches a human when the reporting path is also down.

- Open item for RQ2 — to resolve with Michael: quantization/pruning don't apply to Isolation Forest the same way they do to the Dense Autoencoder (no weights to compress), so an operational definition is needed (e.g., tree-count/depth reduction as the pruning analog) before RQ2's grid can treat every baseline candidate consistently.

- Agenda for 01.10.2026 meeting with Michael: resolve the two open items above, confirm RQ4's scope/ambition level, then move into methodology and data preprocessing.

**Status Update 10.08.2026**

**Context — four related data files**

- **incident_events.csv — GWDG’s full operator log: 69 incidents, 26 nodes. Ground truth dates/descriptions. Exists independently of telemetry.**

- **manifest.csv — catalog of the 21 files ****actually extracted**** (16 incident + 5 healthy reference), covering only 7 nodes.**

- **meta.json**** — per-file cleaning record (which metrics were kept, ****raw→tidy**** lineage).**

- **telemetry CSV — the actual rows (****timeUtc****, node, metric, value, ****gpu****, device…), long format.**

**Key figures from incident_events.csv EDA**

- **Date range: 2025-01-07 to 2026-02-12 (401 days)**

- **Total incidents: 69, across 44 unique incident days**

- **Unique nodes: 26, of which 11 have multiple incidents, Telemetry available for 7 unique ****nodes(****3 nodes have multiple incidents, 4 have single incidents)**

- **Unique categories: 8; ****gpu**** error/problem (31) and ****gpu**** fell off bus (24) are the most frequent, Telemetry available for 3 categories: ****gpu**** error/****problem(****5/31), ****gpu**** fell off bus (7/24), ****gpu**** ****lost(****3/3)**

- **why ****other categories were**** excluded like “****gpu**** unknown” which had 5 incidents?**

- **Nodes with multiple incidents: 11 nodes account for 54 of 69 total incidents. On average, ~79% of a repeat node’s incidents share the same category — most nodes lean toward one dominant failure type rather than a mix.**

- **Telemetry-covered repeat nodes specifically: average same-category consistency is higher, ~87% (2 of the 3 covered nodes are 100% single-category).**

**Same-day clustering pattern**

- **15 dates had multiple different nodes reporting incidents simultaneously, totaling 40 incidents**

- **On 30 of those 40 (75%), the same category appeared across the different nodes on that ****date — most frequently**** ****gpu**** error/problem (20) and ****gpu**** fell off bus (17)**

