**Status Update 30.07.2026 - x**

**Agenda**

- Did you review recent literature on log anomaly detection for distributed systems and HPC environments?

- please write your findings here in points

- Statistical and rule-based methods

- Classical machine learning (e.g., Isolation Forest, One-Class SVM)

- Deep learning methods (LSTM, GRU, Transformers, Autoencoders)

- Online Anomaly Detection in HPC Systems([https://arxiv.org/pdf/1902.08447](https://pad.gwdg.de/_link?url=https%3A%2F%2Farxiv.org%2Fpdf%2F1902.08447&note=Etgms5l1RayhbrPnIm64EA))

- Trains one sparse autoencoder per compute node on ~2 months of normal-only telemetry (166 features: power, temp, frequency, GPU usage, etc.) — no fault labels needed, since only healthy data is used for training.

- Core idea: under healthy conditions, features are correlated in predictable ways (e.g. power ↔ workload, temperature ↔ clock frequency); autoencoder learns these correlations, and anomalies are flagged when reconstruction error rises because the correlations no longer hold.

- Design finding relevant to us: per-node models outperformed one shared/generic model (14.54 vs. 6.29 anomalous-period error) — something we could test as a comparison experiment on our 7 GPU nodes: one model per node vs. one model for all nodes.

- achieve lightweight deployment through a small architecture and dedicated hardware, not through quantization or pruning — our method could differ by applying compression (quantization/pruning) to a heavier model

- Resource-efficient AI techniques (quantization, pruning, TinyML)

- Quantization reduces the precision of each number in the model (fewer bits per weight); pruning reduces the number of weights in the model (removing near-zero/unimportant connections). Both shrink the model, but they save different resources — quantization mainly speeds up computation and cuts memory/energy, while pruning mainly shrinks storage size.

- Lightweight and Energy-Aware Intrusion Detection for Industrial IoT Using TinyML and Edge AI(https://www.mdpi.com/2079-9292/15/13/2879)

- Intrusion detection model runs at 12–18ms per sample on a GPU workstation but 120–180ms per sample on a Raspberry Pi 4, a roughly 10x slowdown that motivates compression techniques like quantization and pruning rather than direct deployment of the full model.

- Lossless Reconstruction of Convolutional Neural Network for Channel-Based Network Pruning([https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9959087/](https://pad.gwdg.de/_link?url=https%3A%2F%2Fpmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov%2Farticles%2FPMC9959087%2F&note=Etgms5l1RayhbrPnIm64EA))

- Compressing a large model produces better results than a well-designed small model with the same resources.

- Experimental implementation of a neural network optical channel equalizer in restricted hardware using pruning and quantization([https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-12563-0](https://pad.gwdg.de/_link?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nature.com%2Farticles%2Fs41598-022-12563-0&note=Etgms5l1RayhbrPnIm64EA))

- Quantization and pruning together reduced model complexity by 78.34% (from 75.96M to 16.45M BOPs) and model size by 87.12% (from 201.4 KB to 25.9 KB), while costing only about 4% accuracy — but most of that complexity reduction came from quantization alone, which by itself accounted for a 69.3% drop (75.96M → 23.32M BOPs), with pruning contributing comparatively little on top.

- A point which can be highlighted here is that:

- Most state-of-the-art deep learning models achieve strong detection performance but require significant computational resources, making them less suitable for deployment on edge devices.

- The focus of the thesis will remain on evaluating the trade-off between anomaly detection performance and resource efficiency, then, in case we had time or you are interested, we can propose a novel machine learning algorithm.

- The edge deployment should demonstrate the feasibility of lightweight inference under constrained hardware resources, using metrics such as model size, inference latency, memory consumption, and CPU utilization.

